      "strategy": "direct",
      "proposed_proof": "**Given:** The covariant metric theory, its chosen first-variation potential,\nand Noether current from the Definition, with the additional algebraic\ncurvature and canonical-representative hypotheses in the Statement.\n\n[A1] [[def-tgrx-noether-horizon-data]]: $\\delta\\mathcal L=E_\\phi[\\delta\\phi]+d\\theta(\\phi;\\delta\\phi)$, $J_\\zeta=\\theta(\\phi;\\mathcal L_\\zeta\\phi)-\\iota_\\zeta\\mathcal L$, and the selected on-shell potential is a globally compatible local covariant primitive.\n\n[A2] [[def-tgrx-noether-horizon-data]]: the Lagrangian is diffeomorphism-covariant, so pullback of the fields pulls back the Lagrangian, and the chosen first-variation decomposition is covariant.\n\n[F1] [[def-lie-derivative-of-a-tensor-field]]: the Lie derivative of a tensor is the derivative of its pullback by the local flow; in covariant components it contains the directional term $\\zeta^e\\nabla_eT$ and one $\\nabla\\zeta$ term for each tensor index.\n\n[F2] [[def-lie-derivative-of-a-differential-form]]: the Lie derivative of a differential form is the derivative of its pullback by the local flow.\n\n[F3] [[thm-cartans-magic-formula]]: $\\mathcal L_\\zeta\\alpha=d(\\iota_\\zeta\\alpha)+\\iota_\\zeta(d\\alpha)$.\n\n\n\n1.1 Differentiating the coordinate curvature tensor and using that the variation of a connection is a tensor gives $$\\delta R^a{}_{bcd}=2\\nabla_{[c}\\delta\\Gamma^a{}_{d]b},\\qquad \\delta\\Gamma^a{}_{bc}=\\tfrac12g^{ae} (\\nabla_b\\delta g_{ec}+\\nabla_c\\delta g_{eb}-\\nabla_e\\delta g_{bc}).$$ Substitute these identities into $P^{abcd}\\delta R_{abcd}$, use the Riemann symmetries of $P$, and apply the product rule to move derivatives off $\\delta g$. The part of the resulting potential that contains a derivative of $\\delta g$ is $$2(\\mathbf E_R^{bcd})\\nabla_d\\delta g_{bc},\\qquad (\\mathbf E_R^{bcd})_{a_2\\ldots a_n} :=P^{abcd}(\\boldsymbol\\epsilon_g)_{a a_2\\ldots a_n};$$ all other curvature terms have no derivative of $\\delta g$. For each finite matter-jet term the repeated identity $$A^a\\nabla_a(\\delta T)=\\nabla_a(A^a\\delta T) -(\\nabla_aA^a)\\delta T$$ moves its finitely many derivatives off the varied tensor slots. Iterating that identity $r$ times writes a term with $r$ derivatives of a variation as a divergence whose coefficients multiply variations of covariant jets of orders $0$ through $r-1$, plus an Euler term with no derivative on the variation. The connection-variation terms are included when those jets are varied as tensors. This supplies a covariant first-variation potential linear in the variations of covariant tensor slots. [A1, algebra]\n\n2.1 By [A2], covariance and the first-variation identity with variation $\\mathcal L_\\zeta\\phi$ give $\\mathcal L_\\zeta\\mathcal L=E_\\phi[\\mathcal L_\\zeta\\phi]+d\\theta(\\phi;\\mathcal L_\\zeta\\phi)$. By [F2], $\\mathcal L_\\zeta\\mathcal L=d(\\iota_\\zeta\\mathcal L)+\\iota_\\zeta(d\\mathcal L)$; since $\\mathcal L$ is an $n$-form on an $n$-manifold, $d\\mathcal L=0$. Subtracting gives $dJ_\\zeta=-E_\\phi[\\mathcal L_\\zeta\\phi]$, so the current is closed on a solution and the assumed canonical local primitive is compatible with the on-shell identity. For a tensor $T$, [F1] gives $$(\\mathcal L_\\zeta T)^{a_1\\ldots a_p}{}_{b_1\\ldots b_q}=\\zeta^e\\nabla_eT^{a_1\\ldots a_p}{}_{b_1\\ldots b_q}-\\sum_{i=1}^p(\\nabla_e\\zeta^{a_i})T^{a_1\\ldots e\\ldots a_p}{}_{b_1\\ldots b_q}+\\sum_{j=1}^q(\\nabla_{b_j}\\zeta^e)T^{a_1\\ldots a_p}{}_{b_1\\ldots e\\ldots b_q}.$$ Thus every covariant tensor-slot contribution to $\\theta(\\phi;\\mathcal L_\\zeta\\phi)$ contains only $\\zeta$ and $\\nabla\\zeta$. The metric slot contributes $2\\mathbf E_R^{bcd}\\nabla_d(\\nabla_b\\zeta_c+\\nabla_c\\zeta_b)$. Since $\\mathbf E_R^{bcd}$ is antisymmetric in $c,d$, $\\mathbf E_R^{bcd}\\nabla_d\\nabla_c\\zeta_b=\\tfrac12\\mathbf E_R^{bcd}[\\nabla_d,\\nabla_c]\\zeta_b$, a curvature-times-$\\zeta$ term. Therefore the sole second-derivative term in $J_\\zeta$ is $2\\mathbf E_R^{bcd}\\nabla_d\\nabla_b\\zeta_c$; $-\\iota_\\zeta\\mathcal L$ contains only $\\zeta$. [A1, A2, F1, F2, F3, algebra, step 1.1]\n\n3.1 Apply the defining integration-by-parts construction of the canonical local representative to the term in step 2.1. The product rule gives $$2\\mathbf E_R^{bcd}\\nabla_d\\nabla_b\\zeta_c =2\\nabla_d(\\mathbf E_R^{bcd}\\nabla_b\\zeta_c) -2(\\nabla_d\\mathbf E_R^{bcd})\\nabla_b\\zeta_c.$$ In form components, the second-derivative part of $(d(X^{cd}\\nabla_{[c}\\zeta_{d]}))_{a_2\\ldots a_n}$ is $$-(n-1)P^{pqcd}(\\boldsymbol\\epsilon_g)_{pq[a_3\\ldots a_n} \\nabla_{a_2]}\\nabla_{[c}\\zeta_{d]}.$$ Antisymmetrizing $p,q$, $c,d$, and the form slots, then exchanging the curvature-derivative index pairs of $P$, gives $$2P^{abcd}(\\boldsymbol\\epsilon_g)_{a a_2\\ldots a_n} \\nabla_d\\nabla_b\\zeta_c,$$ which is the second-derivative term in step 2.1. Thus the coefficient and sign of $X$ are fixed as stated. The remaining terms in the product rule, the curvature commutator, and the other terms of $J_\\zeta$ contain at most first derivatives of $\\zeta$. Repeatedly apply $$A^a{}_b\\nabla_a\\zeta^b=\\nabla_a(A^a{}_b\\zeta^b) -(\\nabla_aA^a{}_b)\\zeta^b$$ to the lower-order terms in the canonical construction. Each step lowers the derivative order on $\\zeta$ by one, so the residual potential term is linear in $\\zeta$, denoted $W^c\\zeta_c$. This proves the canonical formula. The stipulated Lagrangian and potential changes contribute respectively $\\iota_\\zeta\\mu$ and $\\mathbf Y(\\phi;\\mathcal L_\\zeta\\phi)$ to the charge; an exact charge change contributes $dZ$. This gives the full stated class. [A1, A2, F1, F2, F3, algebra, step 2.1] ∎\n\n\n\nThe global-compatibility condition is separate from the local integration by\nparts: the result does not assert that an arbitrary closed global charge\nshift is exact. The entropy-variation theorem uses only this representative\nclass. Higher-derivative curvature terms require replacing $P$ by the full\ncurvature Euler derivative and a separate statement."
    },
    {
      "file": "items/pthm-tgrx-noether-horizon-entropy-variation.md",
      "item_id": "pthm-tgrx-noether-horizon-entropy-variation",
      "status": "repair",
      "strategy": "direct",
      "proposed_proof": "**Given:** A smooth stationary solution and a smooth linearized perturbation as in the Statement, with the covariant Lagrangian, Noether potential and fixed generator of [[def-tgrx-noether-horizon-data]].\n\n[A1] [[def-tgrx-noether-horizon-data]]: $\\xi$ and $B$ are fixed; $\\mathcal L_\\xi\\phi=0$; the first variation is $\\delta\\mathcal L=E_\\phi[\\delta\\phi]+d\\theta(\\phi;\\delta\\phi)$; $B$ is compact, spacelike and boundaryless; $\\nabla_a\\xi_b=\\kappa\\epsilon_{ab}$ with constant $\\kappa>0$; and $Q_\\xi$ is a smooth covariant Noether potential.\n\n[F1] [[def-lie-derivative-of-a-tensor-field]] and [[def-lie-derivative-of-a-differential-form]]: Lie derivatives are derivatives of pullbacks by the local flow; covariance of $\\theta$ makes its field variation under the fixed flow equal to its Lie derivative.\n\n[F2] [[thm-cartans-magic-formula]]: $\\mathcal L_X\\omega=d(\\iota_X\\omega)+\\iota_X(d\\omega)$ for every vector field $X$ and differential form $\\omega$.\n\n[F3] [[thm-stokes-theorem-for-smooth-singular-chains]]: $\\int_c d\\omega=\\int_{\\partial c}\\omega$ for every finite smooth singular chain $c$ and smooth form $\\omega$.\n\n[F4] [[lem-tgrx-noether-charge-decomposition]]: the chosen first-variation potential and charge are in the canonical covariant integration-by-parts class, and $Q_\\xi=W^c\\xi_c+X^{cd}\\nabla_{[c}\\xi_{d]}+\\mathbf Y(\\phi;\\mathcal L_\\xi\\phi)+dZ$, with $X^{cd}_{a_3\\ldots a_n}=-P^{abcd}(\\boldsymbol\\epsilon_g)_{ab a_3\\ldots a_n}$ and only the stated ambiguities.\n\n\n\n1.1 By [F4], the selected charge has the stated $W^c\\xi_c$, $X^{cd}\\nabla_{[c}\\xi_{d]}$, $\\mathbf Y(\\mathcal L_\\xi\\phi)$, and exact-form decomposition. On $B$, $\\xi=0$ and $\\mathcal L_\\xi\\phi=0$. The $W^c\\xi_c$ and $\\mathbf Y$ terms and their field variations vanish there except for $\\mathbf Y(\\phi;\\mathcal L_\\xi\\delta\\phi)$. Covariance identifies this term with $\\mathcal L_\\xi\\mathbf Y(\\phi;\\delta\\phi)$; Cartan's formula [F2] writes it as $\\iota_\\xi d\\mathbf Y+d(\\iota_\\xi\\mathbf Y)$, whose pullback integrates to zero because $\\xi=0$ on $B$ and $B$ has no boundary. The $dZ$ variation integrates to zero by Stokes [F3]. The Lagrangian ambiguity $\\iota_\\xi\\mu$ vanishes on $B$, and the exact charge ambiguity also integrates to zero. Therefore $\\delta\\int_BQ_\\xi=\\delta\\int_BX^{cd}\\nabla_{[c}\\xi_{d]}$. [A1, F1, F2, F3, F4, algebra]\n\n1.2 Define $w_{cd}:=\\nabla_{[c}\\xi_{d]}-\\kappa\\epsilon_{cd}$ along $B$. It vanishes on the background. Since $\\xi$ and $B$ are fixed and $\\xi=0$ on $B$, $\\delta(\\nabla_c\\xi^a)=\\delta\\Gamma^a_{ce}\\xi^e=0$ there. Lowering the index and using $\\nabla_c\\xi^a=\\kappa\\epsilon_c{}^a$ gives $\\delta w_{cd}=-\\kappa g_{a[d}\\delta\\epsilon_{c]}{}^a$. The binormal annihilates tangent vectors, so its variation has no tangent-tangent block; its fixed normalization $\\epsilon^a{}_b\\epsilon^b{}_a=2$ removes the normal-normal antisymmetric block in $g_{a[d}\\delta\\epsilon_{c]}{}^a$. Thus $\\delta w_{cd}$ has only normal-tangent components. The stationary covariant tensor $X$ obeys $\\mathcal L_\\xi X=0$. At a point of $B$, $\\xi=0$, so this equation is invariance under the linear action generated by $\\nabla\\xi=\\kappa\\epsilon$, a Lorentz boost in the normal plane. Its tensor components have total normal boost weight zero, and are invariant when both normal directions are reflected. A component with one normal and one tangent index changes sign under this reflection, so it vanishes. The pullback of $X^{cd}\\delta w_{cd}$ to $B$ is therefore zero. [A1, F1, algebra]\n\n2.1 On the background, $\\nabla_a\\xi_b=\\kappa\\epsilon_{ab}$ and [F4] gives $X^{cd}\\epsilon_{cd}=-P^{abcd}\\epsilon_{ab}\\epsilon_{cd}\\,dA_B$. Write the charge term as $X^{cd}(\\kappa\\epsilon_{cd}+w_{cd})$. Since $w=0$ on the background, its variation is $\\kappa\\delta(X^{cd}\\epsilon_{cd})+X^{cd}\\delta w_{cd}$. The second term integrates to zero by step 1.2; the first includes the variations of $P$, the binormal, and the induced area form. Hence $\\delta\\int_BQ_\\xi=\\kappa\\,\\delta\\int_BX^{cd}\\epsilon_{cd}=-\\kappa\\,\\delta\\int_BP^{abcd}\\epsilon_{ab}\\epsilon_{cd}\\,dA_B=(\\hbar\\kappa/(2\\pi k_B))\\,\\delta S_W$. The coefficient $\\kappa$ is the fixed background surface gravity, so no $\\delta\\kappa$ term appears. [step 1.1, step 1.2, algebra] ∎\n\n\nThe sign is fixed by the displayed curvature, binormal, and Noether-current conventions. Higher-derivative curvature terms require replacing $P$ by the full curvature Euler derivative. This stationary identity provides no dynamical monotonicity theorem and no thermodynamic interpretation without a separate physical assumption."
    },
    {
      "file": "items/pthm-tgrx-noether-first-law.md",
      "item_id": "pthm-tgrx-noether-first-law",
      "status": "repair",
      "strategy": "direct",
      "proposed_proof": "**Given:** The stationary Noether data, the compact boundaryless bifurcation surface and the admitted smooth linearized perturbation in the Statement.\n\n[A1] [[def-tgrx-noether-horizon-data]]: $J_\\xi=\\theta(\\phi;\\mathcal L_\\xi\\phi)-\\iota_\\xi\\mathcal L$, $C_\\xi=J_\\xi-dQ_\\xi$, $\\mathcal L_\\xi\\phi=0$, $E_\\phi=0$, and $\\delta C_\\xi=0$. The boundary one-form is exactly $\\lim_R\\int_{S_R}(\\delta Q_\\xi-\\iota_\\xi\\theta)$ and satisfies $d_{\\mathcal S}H_\\xi=\\alpha_\\xi$.\n\n[F1] [[thm-cartans-magic-formula]]: $\\mathcal L_\\xi\\alpha=d(\\iota_\\xi\\alpha)+\\iota_\\xi d\\alpha$ for every differential form $\\alpha$.\n\n[F2] [[thm-stokes-theorem-for-smooth-singular-chains]]: $\\int_{C_R}d\\alpha=\\int_{\\partial C_R}\\alpha$ for each finite smooth singular chain $C_R$ and smooth form $\\alpha$.\n\n[F3] [[lem-tgrx-noether-charge-decomposition]]: the selected charge is in the local representative class with $X^{cd}_{a_3\\ldots a_n}=-P^{abcd}(\\boldsymbol\\epsilon_g)_{ab a_3\\ldots a_n}$.\n\n[L1] [[pthm-tgrx-noether-horizon-entropy-variation]]: for that representative, $\\delta\\int_BQ_\\xi=(\\hbar\\kappa/(2\\pi k_B))\\delta S_W$.\n\n\n\n1.1 Differentiate $J_\\xi$ at fixed $\\xi$. Since $\\mathcal L_\\xi\\phi=0$ on the stationary background and $\\theta$ is linear in its variation argument, $$\\delta J_\\xi=\\theta(\\phi;\\delta(\\mathcal L_\\xi\\phi))-\\iota_\\xi\\delta\\mathcal L =\\theta(\\phi;\\mathcal L_\\xi\\delta\\phi)-\\iota_\\xi\\bigl(E_\\phi[\\delta\\phi]+d\\theta(\\phi;\\delta\\phi)\\bigr).$$ The first equality uses that the coefficient variation of $\\theta(\\phi;0)$ is zero; the second uses $\\delta(\\mathcal L_\\xi\\phi)=\\mathcal L_\\xi\\delta\\phi$ because $\\xi$ is fixed. Covariance of $\\theta$ identifies $\\theta(\\phi;\\mathcal L_\\xi\\delta\\phi)=\\mathcal L_\\xi\\theta(\\phi;\\delta\\phi)$. Since $E_\\phi=0$, Cartan's identity [F1] yields $\\delta J_\\xi=d(\\iota_\\xi\\theta)$. [A1, F1, algebra]\n\n2.1 By [A1], $C_\\xi=J_\\xi-dQ_\\xi$ and $\\delta C_\\xi=0$. Differentiating this identity gives $\\delta J_\\xi=d(\\delta Q_\\xi)$ because $d$ is independent of the field variation and the spacetime coordinates. Combining with step 1.1 gives $d(\\delta Q_\\xi-\\iota_\\xi\\theta)=0$. This conclusion uses the explicit constraint-variation premise; it is not inferred from the closure of a presymplectic current. [A1, step 1.1, algebra]\n\n3.1 Apply [F2] to $\\alpha=\\delta Q_\\xi-\\iota_\\xi\\theta$ on $C_R$. By step 2.1 and $\\partial C_R=S_R-B$, $$0=\\int_{C_R}d\\alpha=\\int_{S_R}(\\delta Q_\\xi-\\iota_\\xi\\theta)-\\int_B(\\delta Q_\\xi-\\iota_\\xi\\theta).$$ On $B$, $\\xi=0$, so $\\iota_\\xi\\theta=0$. Taking the limit in the first term is permitted by the boundary convergence assumption in [A1], and its value is $\\delta H_\\xi$ by the specified definition of the Hamiltonian variation. Hence $\\delta H_\\xi=\\int_B\\delta Q_\\xi$. [A1, F2, step 2.1, algebra]\n\n4.1 By [F3], the charge used here is in the representative class required by [L1]. Apply [L1] to the right-hand side of step 3.1. This gives $\\delta H_\\xi=(\\hbar\\kappa/(2\\pi k_B))\\delta S_W$, with all field variations taken at fixed generator and fixed background $\\kappa$. [F3, step 3.1, L1]\n\n5.1 For $\\ell_{\\rm EH}=R/(16\\pi G)$, the curvature convention in the preceding entropy-variation theorem gives $R=g^{ac}g^{bd}R_{abcd}$. Holding $g$ fixed and using $\\delta R_{abcd}=-\\delta R_{abdc}$, the Riemann-symmetric derivative is $$P^{abcd}=\\frac{1}{32\\pi G}(g^{ac}g^{bd}-g^{ad}g^{bc}).$$ Its binormal contraction is $P^{abcd}\\epsilon_{ab}\\epsilon_{cd}=(-2-2)/(32\\pi G)=-1/(8\\pi G)$, because $\\epsilon_{ab}\\epsilon^{ab}=-2$ and $\\epsilon_{ab}\\epsilon^{ba}=2$. Substitution in the definition of $S_W$ yields $S_W=k_B\\int_BdA_B/(4G\\hbar)=k_BA_B/(4G\\hbar)$. [L1, algebra] ∎\n\n\n\nThe stationary relation is a model deduction. Calling $S_W$ the physical thermodynamic state entropy requires an additional physical identification; the restricted Kerr–Newman assumption on the earlier thermodynamic page is not generalized here."
    },
    {
      "file": "items/ex-tgrx-fr-stationary-entropy.md",
      "item_id": "ex-tgrx-fr-stationary-entropy",
      "status": "repair",
      "strategy": "direct",
      "proposed_proof": "**Given:** The algebraic-curvature stationary data and $C^1$ function $f$ in the Example.\n\n[F1] [[pthm-tgrx-noether-horizon-entropy-variation]]: for an algebraic-curvature Lagrangian, $S_W=-(2\\pi k_B/\\hbar)\\int_B P^{abcd}\\epsilon_{ab}\\epsilon_{cd}\\,dA_B$.\n\n[F2] The curvature convention and scalar $R=g^{ac}g^{bd}R_{abcd}$ are those explicitly fixed in the Statement of [[pthm-tgrx-noether-horizon-entropy-variation]].\n\n\n\n**Given:** The Lagrangian and curvature convention of the Example and [F1]–[F2].\n\n1.1 Holding $g$ fixed and varying only the algebraic curvature slot gives $$\\delta\\ell=\\frac{f'(R)}{16\\pi G}\\delta R =\\frac{f'(R)}{32\\pi G}(g^{ac}g^{bd}-g^{ad}g^{bc})\\delta R_{abcd},$$ because $\\delta R_{abcd}=-\\delta R_{abdc}$ and exchanging $c,d$ in the second summand changes its sign. Thus $P^{abcd}=\\frac{f'(R)}{32\\pi G}(g^{ac}g^{bd}-g^{ad}g^{bc})$. [F1, F2, algebra]\n\n2.1 Contracting with the normalized binormal gives $$P^{abcd}\\epsilon_{ab}\\epsilon_{cd} =\\frac{f'(R)}{32\\pi G}\\bigl(\\epsilon^{cd}\\epsilon_{cd}-\\epsilon^{dc}\\epsilon_{cd}\\bigr) =-\\frac{f'(R)}{8\\pi G},$$ since $\\epsilon^{cd}\\epsilon_{cd}=-2$ and $\\epsilon^{dc}\\epsilon_{cd}=2$. Substitute this pointwise contraction into [F1] and integrate over $B$ to obtain the displayed expression. The integral allows $R$ and $f'(R)$ to vary over the horizon. [F1, step 1.1, algebra] ∎"
    },
